<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gwalior Fort</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gopachal, Gopadri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gwalior Fort is a hill fort above the city of Gwalior in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, one of the strongest fortresses of northern India. Held by successive dynasties, including the Kachchhapaghatas and Tomaras, it contains the Teli ka Mandir and Sas-Bahu temples, Jain rock-cut sculptures and the 15th-century Man Mandir palace. A temple inscription of 876 shows one of the earliest written zeros.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gwalior-fort.jpg" alt="Gwalior Fort" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anuppyr007 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gwalior_Fort_front.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The date of the fort's construction is unknown. A local legend tells of a king, Suraj Sen, who around the third century CE was cured of leprosy by a sage named Gwalipa; the king walled the sacred pond whose water healed him, built the fort and named it after the sage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> inscriptions call the hill Gopgiri or Gopachala Durg, names meaning cowherd's hill. An inscription of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huna-people">Huna</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihirakula">Mihirakula</a> records a sun temple built there in the 6th century, and the fort certainly existed by the 8th century, when historical records first mention it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>From the 11th century Muslim dynasties attacked the fort. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> besieged it for four days in 1022 and, according to the Tabaqat-i-Akbari, withdrew for a tribute of 35 elephants. Bahauddin Tourghil, a slave commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a>, took it in 1196, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iltutmish">Iltutmish</a> recaptured it in 1232. The Tomars held it from 1398; Sikander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> failed to take it in 1505, but after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a>'s attack of 1516, in which Maan Singh died, the Tomars surrendered following a year-long siege. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> took the fort within a decade, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a> in 1542 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> in 1558.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> made the fort a prison for political prisoners. The Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> held the young Sikh Guru Hargobind there from 1609; Sikh tradition places his release on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwali">Diwali</a>, now marked as Bandi Chhor Divas. Murad Bakhsh and Sulaiman Shikoh were executed in the Man Mandir palace under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>. The fort then passed between the Ranas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gohad">Gohad</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and the British East India Company, whose men took it in a night raid on 3 August 1780, before the British handed it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scindias">Scindias</a> in 1886.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The fort stands on Gopachal, a long, narrow ridge of Vindhyan sandstone overlain by basalt, and its ramparts follow the edge of the hill, linked by six bastions. The main entrance is the Hathi Pol, or Elephant gate, the last of a series of seven. The Man Mandir is known as the Painted Palace for its turquoise, green and yellow tiles, and the Gurjari Mahal, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-tomar">Man Singh Tomar</a> for his wife Mrignayani, is now an archaeological museum. The Teli ka Mandir, built by the Pratihara emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihira-bhoja">Mihira Bhoja</a>, is the oldest part of the fort, and the Saas Bahu temples date from 1092–93.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gwalior Fort” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An inscription of 876 at the Chaturbhuj temple in <b>Gwalior Fort</b>, set up under Mihira Bhoja, contains one of the earliest known inscribed zeros in India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>According to a Gwalior inscription, Vajradaman took the fort of <b>Gopadri</b>, Gwalior, from the Pratiharas around 977.</blockquote><a href="/state/kachchhapaghata-dynasty">Kachchhapaghata dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
